---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="How this site and its backlinks work.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="hero__eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>How Vadin's blog is kept</h1>
      <p>
        This site is built with <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Astro</a> and published as plain static HTML — no server, no database. Every post is a Markdown
        file in <code>src/content/blog/</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Writing a backlink</h2>
      <p>
        There's no special syntax to learn. Link to another post the normal Markdown way, using
        its site-relative URL:
      </p>
      <pre><code>See my earlier note on [tide pools](/blog/tide-pools/).</code></pre>
      <p>
        At build time, a small script reads every post's source, looks for links that point at
        <code>/blog/&lt;slug&gt;</code>, and builds a reverse index. That's what powers the
        "Referenced by" list at the bottom of each entry — it always reflects the current state
        of the content, and there's nothing to keep in sync by hand.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this design</h2>
      <p>
        A backlink is more honest than a tag: it says exactly which piece of writing thought
        this one was worth mentioning, and lets you follow that thread in either direction.
        Over time the posts stop reading like a stream and start reading like a small,
        cross-referenced index — closer to a library card catalog than a feed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
